WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.541
Every Wonder Woman screen adaptation reveals
something about what Wonder Woman means to

00:00:04.541 --> 00:00:07.000
the world and just how messy that meaning
can be.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.170
Join us as we explore the highs and lows of
the Amazon princess, from 1960s homebody to

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:17.420
2000s career girl, as we rank every screen
adaptation of Wonder Woman from worst to best.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.160
What do people love about Wonder Woman?

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.300
Is it her bravery, her ceaseless commitment
to good, her fighting prowess?

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:29.190
The minds behind the canceled 2011 Wonder
Woman TV series starring Adrianne Palicki

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.920
decided it was none of the above Rather, as
the unaired pilot argues, people like Wonder

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.289
Woman because beneath the lasso and bracelets,
she's just a silly bachelorette who wants

00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:42.179
to flop onto her couch with her cat and a
glass of wine to catch a screening of The

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:42.680
Notebook.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.200
It's not all bad, exactly.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.620
Though the series takes some serious left
turns in imagining Diana as the CEO of "Themyscira

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.089
Industries," a company we see as chiefly concerned
with selling buxom dolls of their vigilante

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:58.300
boss, it's also not afraid to show her eager
to do what's right and stand up against her

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.300
own objectification.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.520
"I never signed off on this prototype.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.460
I hadn't seen it, I never would have done
that, I never said to merchandise my t---."

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.240
But the overwhelming impression is that of
a series embarrassed by its own source material.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.320
Add in a limp lead performance from the usually
reliable Palicki and some truly clumsy camera

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.700
work, and you get the most disappointing Wonder
Woman adaptation of all time.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.640
Audiences worldwide might remember Lynda Carter's
twirling, tiara-sporting Wonder Woman as an

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.310
emblem of 1970s entertainment, but they're
significantly less likely to recall her 1974

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.300
predecessor.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.220
That Wonder Woman was played by tennis pro
Cathy Lee Crosby, only one year before Carter's

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.920
Amazon princess would take the small screen
by storm.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.380
Why did one iteration so eclipse the other?

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.780
Well, right off the bat there's Crosby herself.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.860
Clad in a reinterpretation of Wonder Woman's
classic costume that looked more like a fleece

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:52.979
ski vest than anything else, this Diana wasn't
much more than a generic 70s style kung-fu

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.220
fighter - pitted against villains no one remembers
today.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.360
Of the many liberties taken, not one of them
improved upon the classic canon.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:05.229
Years later, Crosby's Wonder Woman is mostly
a footnote in the character's history a justified

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:08.040
fate for an enterprise so alienated from its
origins.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.920
Before Lynda Carter or Cathy Lee Crosby, there
was another Diana Prince.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.410
She was played by Ellie Wood Walker, and boy,
was she in dire straits.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.380
Little is known about this failed Wonder Woman
adaptation: all that's been left behind is

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.720
five minutes of footage.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.100
And really, Walker does an admirable job with
the material she's given.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.210
A 27-year-old still living with her mother,
she's bullied for being unmarried until she

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.830
realizes the thunderstorm raging outside will
prevent Steve Trevor still a pilot in this

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.060
incarnation, apparently from taking off.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.160
"Steve's plane must be grounded."

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.680
She heads behind a revolving door, only to
emerge as Linda Harrison in a Wonder Woman

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.200
costume.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.780
As a 1966 Batman-style take on Wonder Woman,
it sort of works, and Walker manages to bury

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.340
the seeds of what could have been a genuinely
funny performance in those scant five minutes.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.850
But the angle makes no sense.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.280
What does making Wonder Woman so silly accomplish?

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.100
Why not play her absurdities straight, as
Batman did to campy greatness?

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.100
We'll never know what might have been, and
perhaps that's for the best.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.040
Amazons are warriors.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.950
This has been part of their history since
the Greek mythology that helped inspire Wonder

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.670
Woman's creation, and has always been one
of the character's essential attributes.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:19.200
But before she is a wager of war, she is a
scholar, a dreamer, and a seeker of truth.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.810
As she once noted in the pages of her comics,
Amazons "don't raise [their] hand at all until

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:24.860
[they've] first extended it."

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.360
The Wonder Woman of Justice League: War and
the other direct-to-DVD DC animated movies

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.120
that share its continuity might beg to differ.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.210
She is absolutely fearsome with her fists,
the kind of hero who stands before any angry

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.060
crowd and asks for the man responsible for
their anger:

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.020
"Tell me who you speak of, and by Zeus, they
will taste my steel."

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.200
It can be thrilling to watch such an action-minded
Wonder Woman, and many of her fight scenes

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.680
are impeccably choreographed and animated.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.980
But it's tiring, after a while, to see her
constantly with sword in hand.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.780
Add in a weak romance with Superman, and this
Wonder Woman feels more like a petulant adolescent's

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.980
idea of "cool" than a hero with history and
a mission.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.680
Based on Darwyn Cooke's classic limited comics
series, The New Frontier blends the militaristic

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.570
machinations of Cold War policy with the bright-eyed
idealism of World War II-era superheroism,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.680
and the animated adaptation largely lives
up to its source material.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.240
But when it comes to Wonder Woman, those successes
are a bit more muddied.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.720
This Wonder Woman has been beaten down by
postwar disillusionment.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.580
Once, perhaps, she was a hopeful dreamer full
of can-do spirit and Themysciran wisdom.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.300
Now she allows women caught in the midst of
the Vietnam War to kill their abusers.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:36.040
It's an interesting moral argument, and Lucy
Lawless' steely performance is superb.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:40.080
But with scenes like the one that concludes
with her directing a judgmental Superman to

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.740
the door, one in which her fellow Amazons
deride her leadership, and one when she crash-lands

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.840
her blood-splattered jet into a military base,
it's all a little dire for a character so

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.420
closely aligned with optimism.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.780
It's an interesting departure from the character's
history, but not one to be indulged for very

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:55.280
long.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.120
As far as superheroes go, Batman has the biggest
part to play in The Lego Movie.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.031
But he's not the only DC hero pounding Bricksburg's
pavement Wonder Woman is there too, lasso

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.760
in hand, as capable in plastic as she is in
the flesh.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.660
For, well, all of 30 seconds.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.250
True, she's not a major part of The Lego Movie,
with all of two lines that add up to a combined

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:14.960
eight words.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.660
"To the invisible jet… dang it."

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.540
But the sheer fact of her existence within
the candy-colored confines of The Lego Movie's

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.180
world is enough to merit her a place on this
list.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.600
Here, Wonder Woman is a Master Builder, a
being of unparalleled creativity and vision.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.861
Though the movie's moral is, ultimately, that
anyone can be a Master Builder, audiences

00:05:33.861 --> 00:05:37.780
are informed that the ones who earned the
title pre-movie worked for years "to clear

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.800
their minds enough to have even a fleeting
glimpse of the Man Upstairs."

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.020
Now ask yourself: who can't be charmed by
the idea of a Wonder Woman who guards creativity

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:49.260
itself from a literal patriarch, bent on keeping
his toys from being played with?

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.740
Wonder Woman is easily the best part of her
2009 animated movie.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.980
She maintains a tireless faith in the world,
her ideals, and her abilities.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.140
Watching her throw down with Ares is thrilling.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:03.820
The sequence in which she steps into her costume
for the first time is stirring.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.660
She's everything you want her to be, really.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.360
The problem has everything to do with the
movie around her.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.760
Wonder Woman seems to believe it needs to
apologize for its stalwart heroine by constantly

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.300
sexualizing her and the culture she comes
from.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.100
This iteration of Steve Trevor is an absolute
sleazeball who can barely go five minutes

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.190
without making it clear that Diana and her
sisters matter most to him as babes.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.950
The fact that their romance succeeds at all
when so much of their interaction consists

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:30.660
of Steve making her uncomfortable lessens
her character.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.920
Would Wonder Woman fall for a man who sneers
at her mother's long-since-past liaison with

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.630
Ares as a stupid woman falling for a "bad
boy," let alone one who refers to her as "the

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.560
hot chick" to her Amazon sisters?

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.260
This movie argues she would, and that's why
this iteration misses the mark.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.540
Alternate universes have well been explored
within the pages of DC Comics and the tradition

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.480
continues to flourish today, in stories like
Justice League: Gods and Monsters.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.300
Within the cynical confines of its DC universe,
Superman is the son of General Zod, Batman

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:05.020
is a literal vampire, and Wonder Woman is
Bekka, a New God from DC's Fourth World comics.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.470
Their approach to enforcing global order is
considerably more brutal than their mainstream

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.580
counterparts consider its Wonder Woman.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:16.020
She might believe in justice, but she's a
lot less into lassos and a lot more into using

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.340
her sword to impale her enemies to achieve
it.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:18.920
"Low tech.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:20.040
Works for me."

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:20.940
Does it work?

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.220
For the most part, yes.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.440
This is only a Wonder Woman depiction in the
most technical sense, but it works on its

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:29.310
own and as an interesting riff off the established
character.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:33.690
Bekka's far-off land is considerably different
from Themyscira, and what sent her to Earth

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.030
the death of her husband, Orion is a lot sadder
than Diana's hopeful mission of peace.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:41.980
But those changes highlight the commitment
to righting wrongs both heroines share, making

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:45.080
this wildly different Wonder Woman a worthy
enough rendition.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.870
Before Batman: The Animated Series, Teen Titans,
and DC Super Hero Girls, there was the ever-cheery

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.360
Super Friends.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.240
Wonder Woman is a particularly bright spot
in this primary-colored collection.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.581
A fairly straightforward depiction, from her
Amazonian heritage to her invisible jet, she

00:08:00.581 --> 00:08:04.940
nevertheless maintains a certain wry edge
that stands out against her teammates.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.800
She's nowhere near sarcastic this is, after
all, the cartoon that gave us the Wonder Twins

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.460
but Shannon Farnon's performance has a knowing
lilt to it.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:17.160
Her Wonder Woman is a whole-hearted hero,
but one can imagine her rolling her eyes good-naturedly,

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:17.660
of course.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.199
Above all, she's a do-gooder dedicated to
righting wrongs in a version of the DC universe

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:26.350
where that means battling killer bees and
teaching important lessons about the dangers

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:27.400
of drag racing.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.500
And darn it if she doesn't manage to put a
smile on your face as she does it.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.220
Pop quiz: What was Wonder Woman's first broadcast
appearance?

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:39.770
If you guessed Super Friends, 1974's TV film,
or the series starring Lynda Carter, you're

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:44.860
wrong: it was in "It's All Greek to Me," a
1972 episode of the animated The Brady Bunch

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.290
spinoff The Brady Kids.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:50.540
"It's All Greek To Me" sends the Brady kids
to Ancient Greece alongside Diana Prince,

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.720
mild-mannered administrative assistant in
the local university's mathematics department.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:59.220
She's a bit of a schoolmarm here, her superheroic
persona played for laughs against her buttoned-up

00:08:59.220 --> 00:08:59.960
alter ego.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:04.200
"As Diana Prince, I should say that brains
are more important.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.580
But being Wonder Woman, I know the value of
physical conditioning too."

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.000
It works, for the most part: Wonder Woman
as time-traveling babysitter responsible for

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.300
keeping the groovy sextet from making a mess
of history is, if nothing else, certainly

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:18.800
unique.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.259
All in all, it's a fun detour but it's probably
for the best that Wonder Woman only spent

00:09:23.259 --> 00:09:24.439
22 minutes in Brady-land.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:30.180
Every generation gets its DC cartoons, and
one of the biggest ones going today is Justice

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:30.920
League Action.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:36.490
A zippy, slickly animated series that manages
to fit DC's brightest stars in alongside lesser-known

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:40.079
heroes, it takes its share of liberties, but
rarely without cause.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.780
This version of Wonder Woman is, for example,
a bit more hard-edged than others.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:47.560
Physically, she's a brawler wielding a sword
who gets more action scenes than the character

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.360
has historically been granted in ensemble
cartoons.

00:09:50.379 --> 00:09:54.939
Personality-wise, there's a wry, wisecracking
side to her that stands out among her many

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:55.720
incarnations.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:00.360
A lesser cartoon might have translated that
as generically sassy, but Justice League Action

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.759
locates her sense of humor firmly in a warrior
who loves a good sparring session as much

00:10:04.759 --> 00:10:06.240
as she loves keeping the peace.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.679
That said, the feistiness could use a little
lightening now and then, if only to keep the

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.780
character in touch with the joy and love that
make her unique.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.440
Wonder Woman isn't just a warrior, after all
she's a pacifist as well.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.820
1988's Superman cartoon was a boisterous ode
to the Man of Steel.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:26.240
Unsurprisingly, Wonder Women fits right in,
exemplified by the fabulously-named episode

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.139
"Superman and Wonder Woman vs. the Sorceress
of Time."

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:33.619
Superman, Wonder Woman, and a villain who
combines magic and time travel, thrown together

00:10:33.619 --> 00:10:38.100
in the sort of cartoon that opens with a voiceover
explanation of how Superman came to be?

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:44.389
"Strange visitor from another planet, who
came to Earth with powers and abilities far

00:10:44.389 --> 00:10:45.220
beyond those of mortal men.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.880
Superman!"

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.280
That can only succeed.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:52.720
Not only does it make good on that promise,
it provides genuine humor, adventure, and

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:53.420
world-building.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:58.920
Though Paradise Island is only briefly glimpsed,
its pastel-swathed Amazons, pastoral hills,

00:10:58.939 --> 00:11:04.079
and mysterious artifacts establish the world,
its heroine, and her mission in vivid style.

00:11:04.079 --> 00:11:08.179
Diana herself is at the peak of her heroic
game here, as brave and bright-eyed as any

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.240
fan could ask for.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:13.879
She's notably quicker with a quip than most
adaptations depict her, and it's so charming,

00:11:13.879 --> 00:11:15.800
it's a wonder it doesn't happen more often.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.720
This team-up might have been brief, but it's
a delight from beginning to end.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.779
Batman: The Brave and the Bold is an unabashed
love letter to the Silver Age of DC comics,

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.960
which makes it an absolute joy of a cartoon.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:32.060
Villains ensnare hapless civilians with maliciously
magical music, while no DC character, gritty

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:36.900
or otherwise, escapes a generous dose of humor
within these bubbly, brightly-colored confines.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.420
Unsurprisingly, Wonder Woman responded very
well to this treatment.

00:11:40.689 --> 00:11:46.259
Accompanied by an especially groovy reinterpretation
of her 1970s theme, she is every inch the

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.720
laughing crusader of her earliest Golden Age
years.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:53.460
She's the can-do spirit of the Silver Age
personified, minus the ditzy personality that

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:55.779
era forced on the Wonder Woman of the comics.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:59.420
This Diana isn't just a warrior she's genuinely,
thoroughly, wonderful.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.360
Justice League and Justice League Unlimited
are monuments to the power of animation.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:08.780
Their interpretations of DC's lineup forever
scrubbed away whatever memories of the Super

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.569
Friends lingered in the public's mind.

00:12:10.569 --> 00:12:14.260
It wasn't just a success, but a benchmark
for superhero stories period.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.459
Wonder Woman didn't feature quite as largely
as Batman or Superman did, unfortunately.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.040
Yet Susan Eisenberg's performance was strong
enough to make her into a heroine to be admired.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:27.450
Even in the smallest scenes, Eisenberg summoned
a strength to Diana's voice that immediately

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:30.360
established authority, kindness, and confidence.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:31.960
"Heed my words, Faust.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:37.080
If you break your vow, no magic in the universe
will save you from me."

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.020
Her Diana is funny without ever being mean,
brave without showboating, and hopeful without

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:42.520
limit.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:47.700
She is a pillar of everything the League strives
to be a walking, talking encapsulation of

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.340
why superheroes exist at all.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:53.420
This is a Diana who will never run from a
fight, but would die before she picked one.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:58.920
Wonder Woman: Bloodlines crams an absolute
plethora of the Amazon's villains, friends,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.240
and allies into its runtime.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:04.900
It's a bit of a mess, to be frank but when
it does takes the time to focus on its heroine,

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.340
everything else becomes forgivable.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:10.660
Bloodlines' Wonder Woman is exactly what the
character should always be: A lover and a

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:11.160
fighter.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.519
She does not shy away from a battle, nor does
she provoke them, instead seeking peace with

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:19.980
every villain she faces whether they're rampaging
minotaurs or mutated cheetah-women.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.370
This Diana refuses to accept the world she
has been presented with, finding, even in

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:27.540
the most extreme circumstances, that peace
is always possible.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.519
Now that's a wondrous power indeed.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:36.160
Set at a high school in the DC universe, DC
Super Hero Girls reinterprets Wonder Woman,

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:41.489
Batgirl, Green Lantern, Bumblebee, Supergirl,
and Zatanna as besties, crime fighters, and

00:13:41.489 --> 00:13:43.299
occasional detention sufferers.

00:13:43.299 --> 00:13:47.720
Though the stakes here aren't exactly world-shattering,
this narrowing of scope actually allows the

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:53.000
characters room to grow and establish themselves
as scholars, nerds, divas, and warriors.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:56.980
There isn't a weak member of the cast, but
Diana stands out from the very beginning as

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.960
a joyous tribute to all that has made the
character endure.

00:14:00.129 --> 00:14:04.399
A transfer student, she is as passionate about
being called a woman, rather than a girl,

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:05.500
as she is about ice cream.

00:14:05.769 --> 00:14:09.940
Freed from having to carry the burden of being
a rare female character in a sea full of dudes,

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:14.459
she can be a fully rounded character, complete
with flaws and failures, and never lose her

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:14.960
heroism.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:20.040
This Diana isn't just a Wonder Woman, she's
a woman looking to put her ideals into practice.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:23.920
Whether she's playing dodgeball with her friends
or misunderstanding one of the many idioms

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.540
of Man's World, it's a joy to watch her do
it.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:31.120
The 1970s Wonder Woman series is regarded
today as a success, but rarely without an

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.440
invocation of its campy allure.

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.480
Commentators aren't wrong to bring this up.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:38.939
This was, after all, a show featuring Nazi
cattle rustlers.

00:14:38.939 --> 00:14:43.160
A sense of humor and irreverent fun was very
much the point of the show, and it holds up

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:44.140
remarkably well.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.480
Wonder Woman's universe might be live action,
but in its fundamentals, it is very much a

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:48.980
cartoon.

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:53.200
It's tempting to chalk Lynda Carter's appeal
up to her beauty, and perhaps an unconsidered

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:54.620
charm she simply exudes.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.800
But playing her performance off as something
unconscious is to do her a tremendous disservice.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:00.900
"Excuse me, but that's very rude…

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:01.900
“Get outta here, broad!”

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:03.100
“it's also dangerous."

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:07.920
Carter's Diana is a heroine whose belief in
goodness is so ironclad you can see it in

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:11.569
her eyes, whether she's punching out a bad
guy or perched behind a typewriter.

00:15:11.569 --> 00:15:15.380
Her Wonder Woman is a dreamer with her feet
planted firmly in the real world, a warrior

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.600
who hopes that one day there will be no war.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:22.620
Decades after her premiere, Carter's Diana
doesn't just evoke fondness she commands respect

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.360
for an enduringly fine performance.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.040
There was a lot riding on Gal Gadot.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:30.080
The DC films that preceded her had a rocky
go of it.

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:34.320
Superheroine movies were primarily remembered
as a graveyard for the likes of Catwoman and

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:35.320
Elektra.

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:39.400
Gadot didn't just have a hard road ahead of
her — she had a towering mountain to climb.

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:42.120
"As Amazons, this is our duty."

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:47.199
Years after her debut in Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice, her starring role in Wonder

00:15:47.199 --> 00:15:50.809
Woman, and her part in Justice League, it's
easy to forget all that pressure.

00:15:50.809 --> 00:15:53.959
But that's only because Gadot did such an
enormously good job.

00:15:53.959 --> 00:15:58.449
And, if her winning turn in the otherwise
ho-hum Justice League is any indication, promises

00:15:58.449 --> 00:15:59.520
to continue doing so.

00:15:59.749 --> 00:16:04.540
Her Wonder Woman is as fierce as she is cheery,
an eager competitor who comes alive when presented

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:05.480
with a challenge.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:06.480
An immortal war god?

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:10.560
She'll hop on a boat to slay him before you've
even finished explaining his raison d'etre.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.680
A battlefield's no man's land, strewn with
the bodies of the dead?

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:17.480
She'll jump up to cross it, laser-focused
on civilians in need of saving.

00:16:17.489 --> 00:16:19.869
A dessert she's never tried before, like ice
cream?

00:16:19.869 --> 00:16:22.389
She'll thank the vendor like he invented it
just for her.

00:16:22.389 --> 00:16:23.929
As she says in Justice League:

00:16:23.929 --> 00:16:25.720
"What are you?"

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:26.480
"A believer."

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:29.340
And in so doing, she makes us believe in her
too.

